 class=3D"py-1">Figure 3 Geohashing</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>Geohashing has many uses. In our design, we use geohashing for map tilin=
g. For more details on geohashing and its benefits, please refer to [11].</=
p>
<h4 id=3D"map-rendering">Map rendering</h4>
<p>We won=E2=80=99t go into a lot of detail about map rendering here, but i=
t is worth mentioning the basics. One foundational concept in map rendering=
 is tiling. Instead of rendering the entire map as one large custom image, =
the world is broken up into smaller tiles. The client only downloads the re=
levant tiles for the area the user is in and stitches them together like a =
mosaic for display.</p>
<p>There are distinct sets of tiles at different zoom levels. The client ch=
ooses the set of tiles appropriate for the zoom level of the map viewport o=
n the client. This provides the right level of map details without consumin=
g excess bandwidth. To illustrate with an extreme example, when the client =
is zoomed all the way out to show the entire world, we don=E2=80=99t want t=
o have to download hundreds of thousands of tiles for a very high zoom leve=
l. All the details would go to waste. Instead, the client would download on=
e tile at the lowest zoom level, which represents the entire world with a s=
ingle 256x256 pixel image.</p>
<h4 id=3D"road-data-processing-for-navigation-algorithms">Road data process=
ing for navigation algorithms</h4>
<p>Most routing algorithms are variations of Dijkstra=E2=80=99s or A* pathf=
inding algorithms. The exact algorithm choice is a complex topic and we won=
=E2=80=99t go into much detail in this chapter. What is important to note i=
s that all these algorithms operate on a graph data structure, where inters=
ections are nodes and roads are edges of the graph. See Figure 4 for an exa=
mple:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a map overlay showing a graph network super=
imposed on a section of a city map. The base map displays a typical residen=
tial area with streets (labeled 'Rocca Ave,' 'Miller Avenue,' 'Bruce Avenue=
,' 'Grand Ave,' 'Linden Avenue,' and 'Baden Avenue'), buildings represented=
 as light-grey rectangles, and green patches suggesting parks or open space=
s.  Overlaid on this is a graph consisting of eight nodes, depicted as larg=
e red circles, interconnected by thick black lines forming edges.  These no=
des are clustered within a rectangular bounding box. The graph's structure =
appears irregular, not forming a regular grid or lattice. The map's attribu=
tion in the bottom-right corner reads 'Map tiles by Stamen Design, under CC=
