<h3 id=3D"services">Services</h3>
<p>Now that we have discussed the data model, let=E2=80=99s take a close lo=
ok at some of the most important services: location service, map rendering =
service, and navigation service.</p>
<h4 id=3D"location-service-1">Location service</h4>
<p>In the high-level design, we discussed how location service works. In th=
is section, we focus on the database design for this service and also how u=
ser location is used in detail.</p>
<p>In Figure 14, the key-value store is used to store user location data. L=
et=E2=80=99s take a close look.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a simplified system architecture for retrie=
ving user location data.  A mobile user's request initiates the process, re=
presented by a smartphone icon labeled 'Mobile User'. This request is then =
directed to a 'Load Balancer,' depicted as a rectangular box, which distrib=
utes the incoming traffic. The load balancer forwards the request to a 'Loc=
ation Service,' another rectangular box, responsible for processing the loc=
ation request. Finally, the Location Service accesses and retrieves the req=
uired data from a 'User Location DB,' represented by a cylindrical icon sym=
bolizing a database.  The arrows indicate the unidirectional flow of the re=
quest and data from the mobile user through the load balancer, the location=
