><tr><td><strong>key (user_id)</strong></td><td><strong>timestamp</strong><=
/td><td><strong>lat</strong></td><td><strong>long</strong></td><td><strong>=
user_mode</strong></td><td><strong>navigation_mode</strong></td></tr></thea=
d><tbody><tr><td>51</td><td>132053000</td><td>21.9</td><td>89.8</td><td>act=
ive</td><td>driving</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p class=3D"tableCaption">Table 5 Location data</p>
<p><strong>How do we use the user location data?</strong></p>
<p>User location data is essential. It supports many use cases. We use the =
data to detect new and recently closed roads. We use it as one of the input=
s to improve the accuracy of our map over time. It is also an input for liv=
e traffic data.</p>
<p>To support these use cases, in addition to writing current user location=
s in our database, we log this information into a message queue, such as Ka=
fka. Kafka is a unified low-latency, high-throughput data streaming platfor=
m designed for real-time data feeds. Figure 15 shows how Kafka is used in t=
he improved design.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a system architecture diagram illustrating =
the flow of user location data and its processing.  A mobile user's locatio=
n data first passes through a Load Balancer, then to a Location Service whi=
ch stores the data in a User Location DB. This location data is then fed in=
to a Kafka message queue.  Kafka acts as a central hub, distributing the da=
ta to three downstream services: a Traffic Update Service (which writes to =
a Traffic DB), a Machine Learning Service for Personalization (writing to a=
 Personalization DB), and a Routing Tile Processing Service (writing to an =
object storage labeled 'Routing Tiles').  Finally, data also flows from Kaf=
ka to an Analytics service, which stores its results in an Analytics DB.  T=
he dashed-line box groups the Traffic Update Service, Machine Learning Serv=
ice, Routing Tile Processing Service, and Analytics service, suggesting the=
se components form a processing unit consuming data from Kafka." loading=3D=
"lazy" width=3D"750" height=3D"425" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=
=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/system-design-interview/google-ma=
ps/figure-15-location-data-is-used-by-other-services-EREYYC3G.svg" style=3D=
"color: transparent;"></div><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: c=
enter;"><figcaption class=3D"py-1">Figure 15 Location data is used by other=
 services</figcaption></div></figure>
<p>Other services consume the location data stream from Kafka for various u=
se cases. For instance, the live traffic service digests the output stream =
and updates the live traffic database. The routing tile processing service =
improves the map of the world by detecting new or closed roads and updating=
 the affected routing tiles in object storage. Other services can also tap =
into the stream for different purposes.</p>
<h4 id=3D"rendering-map">Rendering map</h4>
<p>In this section, we dive deep into precomputed map tiles and map renderi=
ng optimization. They are primarily inspired by the work of Google Design [=
5].</p>
<h5 id=3D"precomputed-tiles">Precomputed tiles</h5>
<p>As mentioned previously, there are different sets of precomputed map til=
es at various distinct zoom levels to provide the appropriate level of map =
detail to the user, based on the client=E2=80=99s viewport size and zoom le=
vel. Google Maps uses 21 zoom levels (Table 1). This is what we use, as wel=
l.</p>
<p>Level 0 is the most zoomed-out level. The entire map is represented by a=
 single tile of size 256 * 256 pixels.</p>
<p>With each increment of the zoom level, the number of map tiles doubles i=
n both north-south and east-west directions, while each tile stays at 256 *=
 256 pixels. As shown in Figure 16, at zoom level 1, there are 2 x 2 tiles,=
 with a total combined resolution of 512 * 512 pixels. At zoom level 2, the=
re are 4 x 4 tiles, with a total combined resolution of 1024 * 1024 pixels.=
 With each increment, the entire set of tiles has 4x as many pixels as the =
previous level. The increased pixel count provides an increasing level of d=
etail to the user. This allows the client to render the map at the best gra=
nularities depending on the client=E2=80=99s zoom level, without consuming =
excessive bandwidth to download tiles in excessive detail.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a hierarchical quadtree structure illustrat=
ing map tile organization at different zoom levels.  At Zoom level 0, a 256=
px by 256px map is divided into four quadrants, labeled '00', '01', '10', a=
nd '11'.  Each quadrant represents a portion of the world map.  Zoom level =
1 shows each quadrant from level 0 further subdivided into four 256px by 25=
6px tiles, resulting in a 512px by 512px image.  These tiles are again labe=
led using a two-digit binary code, reflecting their position within the par=
ent quadrant.  Zoom level 2 expands this pattern, with each level 1 tile di=
vided into four more, creating a 1024px by 1024px image composed of 64 tile=
s, each labeled with a two-digit binary code.  The labels correspond to geo=
graphical regions (e.g., Europe, Asia, Africa, etc.) at the appropriate zoo=
m level.  Vertical lines indicate the increasing image dimensions (256px, 5=
12px, and 1024px) at each zoom level.  The bottom of the image includes att=
ribution: 'Map tiles by Stamen Design, under CC BY 3.0. Data =C2=A9 OpenStr=
eetMap contributors.'" loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"500" height=3D"915" decodi=
ng=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/_next/image?url=
=3D%2Fimages%2Fcourses%2Fsystem-design-interview%2Fgoogle-maps%2Ffigure-16-=
zoom-levels-QGEJ7C5F.png&amp;w=3D3840&amp;q=3D75" style=3D"color: transpare=
nt;"></div><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center;"><figcapti=
on class=3D"py-1">Figure 16 Zoom levels</figcaption></div></figure>
<h5 id=3D"optimization-use-vectors">Optimization: use vectors</h5>
<p>With the development and implementation of WebGL, one potential improvem=
ent is to change the design from sending the images over the network, to se=
nding the vector information (paths and polygons) instead. The client draws=
 the paths and polygons from the vector information.</p>
<p>One obvious advantage of vector tiles is that vector data compresses muc=
h better than images do. The bandwidth saving is substantial.</p>
<p>A less obvious benefit is that vector tiles provide a much better zoomin=
g experience. With rasterized images, as the client zooms in from one level=
 to another, everything gets stretched and looks pixelated. The visual effe=
ct is pretty jarring. With vectorized images, the client can scale each ele=
ment appropriately, providing a much smoother zooming experience.</p>
<h4 id=3D"navigation-service-1">Navigation service</h4>
<p>Next, let=E2=80=99s deep dive into the navigation service. This service =
is responsible for finding the fastest routes. The design diagram is shown =
in Figure 17.</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a system architecture diagram for a mobile =
navigation application.  A mobile user initiates a request that flows throu=
gh a Navigation Service. This service interacts with a Geocoding Service, w=
hich in turn retrieves data from a Geocoding DB to determine location. The =
Navigation Service then passes the location data to a Route Planner Service=
.  The Route Planner Service uses three subordinate services: a Ranker, a S=
hortest Path Service, and an ETA Service. The Ranker feeds into a Filter Se=
rvice (which can filter out tolls, etc.). The Shortest Path Service accesse=
s Routing Tiles (an object storage) to calculate the optimal route. The ETA=
 Service retrieves traffic information from a Traffic DB, which is updated =
periodically by an Update Traffic Service that draws data from a Location D=
